VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Per-share data not required for cash flow or balance sheet
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On which financial statement is an entity prohibited from presenting per-share amounts under ASC 260-10-45-6?', '["Income statement", "Statement of cash flows", "Statement of comprehensive income", "Separate statement of retained earnings"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-6, per-share amounts shall not be presented in the statement of cash flows. Presenting cash flow on a per-share basis could be misleading because it might imply that cash flow is a valid measure of performance or value. The income statement (option A) is where per-share data must be presented. The standard explicitly restricts cash flow per-share presentation to prevent confusion with accrual-based earnings measures.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Investment companies exempt from ASC 260 per-share guidance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-15-3, which type of entity is explicitly scoped out of the per-share computation guidance because it follows specialized industry reporting?', '["A real estate investment trust (REIT)", "An investment company subject to ASC 946", "A broker-dealer registered with the SEC", "A government-sponsored enterprise"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Investment companies within the scope of ASC 946 are excluded from ASC 260 because they follow specialized per-share guidance under the investment company reporting model (e.g., net asset value per share). ASC 260-10-15-3 explicitly notes this scope exclusion. REITs (option A) are not scoped out of ASC 260; they present per-share data under the standard''s general requirements. Broker-dealers and government-sponsored enterprises are not specifically excluded by ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — S-corp to C-corp conversion: pro forma EPS starting point
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An S-corporation converts to C-corporation status via an IPO on July 1, 2025. Before conversion, it had no public securities and was exempt from per-share reporting. Post-IPO, it has 500,000 publicly traded common shares. For the 2025 annual financial statements, from what date must per-share data be computed?', '["January 1, 2025, because per-share data covers the full fiscal year", "July 1, 2025, because the S-corp was exempt before the IPO", "Only for the fourth quarter, since quarterly reporting begins after listing", "Per-share data is optional until the first full fiscal year as a C-corp"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, once an entity has publicly traded common stock, it must present per-share data for all periods in the income statement. The 2025 annual statements cover January 1 through December 31, so per-share data is computed for the full year. The S-corporation exemption no longer applies retroactively once the entity becomes public. Option B incorrectly truncates the computation period. Option D has no basis in the codification; the requirement is immediate upon public status.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Increasing-rate preferred dividend deduction
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Harwell Corp. has 10,000 shares of increasing-rate preferred stock that pays a $2 per share dividend in Year 1, $4 in Year 2, and $6 in Year 3 and beyond. In Year 1, net income is $500,000 with 100,000 weighted-average common shares. The controller deducts the $20,000 Year 1 dividend from the EPS numerator. Under ASC 260, evaluate the correct amount to deduct.', '["$20,000 (the Year 1 stated dividend)", "$40,000 (the Year 2 rate as a level yield)", "$60,000 (the maximum rate)", "$33,333 (the average of all three rates)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-S99-2, increasing-rate preferred dividends should generally be deducted from income available to common shareholders based on the stated dividend for the current period. The SEC staff guidance indicates that the Year 1 dividend of $20,000 is the amount deducted from the numerator. Option B incorrectly anticipates future rates; the deduction is based on the dividend attributable to the current period, not a level yield approach unless the increasing rate is designed to compensate for a below-market initial rate.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Variable-share contract (fixed dollar obligation)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverton Corp. has a contract requiring it to deliver $900,000 worth of its own common shares to a supplier in 12 months. The average market price during 2025 is $45 per share. The CFO excludes the contract from diluted EPS because the number of shares is variable. Under ASC 260-10-45-45, evaluate whether the CFO''s exclusion is appropriate and determine the effect on diluted EPS.', '["The CFO is correct; variable-share contracts are excluded from diluted EPS", "The contract should be included, adding 20,000 shares to the diluted denominator", "The contract should be included, but only if it is in the money", "The contract is excluded because it represents a liability, not a potential common share"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-45, when the number of shares to be delivered is based on a fixed monetary amount, the contract is included in diluted EPS. The presumption is share settlement. At an average price of $45, the entity would deliver $900,000 / $45 = 20,000 shares to the denominator. The CFO''s exclusion is incorrect (option A). Option C confuses this with an option-type instrument. Option D mischaracterizes the classification; potential shares are included regardless of balance sheet classification.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Entity-election settlement: choosing the most dilutive assumption
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A company issues a hybrid instrument that gives the company (not the holder) the right to settle in either cash or shares upon conversion. The instrument is classified as a liability on the balance sheet. Management states it intends to settle in cash to avoid dilution. Under ASC 260-10-45-46, evaluate the proper diluted per-share treatment.', '["Cash settlement intent controls; the instrument is excluded from diluted per-share computation", "Share settlement must be assumed if it is more dilutive, regardless of management intent", "The instrument is excluded because liability classification precludes per-share inclusion", "The most recent settlement pattern for similar instruments determines the assumption"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-46 and ASC 260-10-55-32, when the entity controls the settlement method, the more dilutive settlement alternative must be reflected in diluted per-share data. Management''s stated intent to settle in cash does not override the requirement to assume the most dilutive outcome. If share settlement produces greater dilution (adding shares to the denominator) than cash settlement (reducing the numerator), share settlement is assumed. Option A incorrectly defers to management intent. Option C confuses balance sheet classification with per-share inclusion rules.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — REIT operating partnership units: per-share treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A publicly traded REIT owns 80% of an operating partnership (OP). The remaining 20% is held by outside limited partners through redeemable OP units that are exchangeable one-for-one into REIT common shares. Under ASC 260, how should these redeemable OP units be reflected in the REIT''s per-share calculations?', '["Excluded entirely because OP unit holders are not common shareholders of the REIT", "Included in diluted per-share data as potential common shares using the if-converted method", "Included in basic per-share data because the units are economically equivalent to common stock", "Included only if the OP units are classified as equity on the REIT''s balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40 and ASC 260-10-55-32, redeemable OP units exchangeable into common shares represent potential common stock. They are included in diluted per-share computations under the if-converted method — the minority interest eliminated is added back to the numerator and the OP units are added to the denominator. Option A ignores the exchange feature. Option C incorrectly places them in basic rather than diluted. Option D conflates balance sheet classification with per-share inclusion criteria.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Entity with only publicly traded preferred (no common): per-share obligation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A corporation has publicly traded preferred stock listed on the NYSE but its common stock is entirely held by a single private equity sponsor and is not publicly traded. Is this entity required to present per-share data under ASC 260?', '["Yes, because any publicly traded equity security triggers the requirement", "No, because only entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock must present per-share data", "Yes, but only on a voluntary basis with simplified disclosures", "No, because private equity-backed companies are exempt from ASC 260"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, the per-share data requirement applies to entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock (e.g., options, warrants, convertible securities). Having only publicly traded preferred stock — without any publicly traded common stock or potential common stock — does not trigger the ASC 260 requirement. Option A overstates the scope; publicly traded preferred alone is insufficient. Option D fabricates a private-equity exemption that does not exist in the codification.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Contingently adjustable conversion ratio tied to EBITDA target
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Dalworth Corp. has $4,000,000 of 5% convertible bonds that currently convert into 80,000 shares. The bond indenture provides that if EBITDA exceeds $10,000,000 in any year, the conversion ratio increases to 100,000 shares. In 2025, EBITDA is $8,500,000. Net income is $3,000,000 and the tax rate is 25%. The CFO includes 100,000 potential shares in diluted EPS. Evaluate the CFO''s approach.', '["Correct; the most dilutive conversion terms always apply", "Incorrect; only 80,000 shares should be used because the EBITDA threshold is not currently met", "Incorrect; the 100,000 shares should be used but no interest is added back", "Correct; contingently issuable shares are always included at the maximum amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares (including contingent conversion adjustments) are included in diluted EPS only if the contingency condition is currently satisfied at the reporting date. Since 2025 EBITDA of $8,500,000 does not meet the $10,000,000 threshold, only the base conversion of 80,000 shares is included. The CFO''s use of 100,000 shares overstates dilution. Option A incorrectly applies the most-favorable-to-holder principle, which applies to existing conversion terms, not unmet contingencies. Option D conflates contingently issuable shares with guaranteed conversion rights.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Gain on bargain purchase in EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornridge Corp. reports net income of $2,400,000, which includes a $200,000 gain on bargain purchase from a business combination. The company has 400,000 weighted-average shares and $100,000 in preferred dividends. An analyst excludes the bargain purchase gain from the EPS numerator, arguing it is a nonrecurring item. Evaluate the analyst''s approach under ASC 260.', '["The analyst is correct; bargain purchase gains are excluded from EPS", "The analyst is incorrect; the full net income of $2,400,000 less preferred dividends is used", "Only 50% of the bargain purchase gain should be included", "The gain is included in basic EPS but excluded from diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-5, the numerator for basic EPS is income available to common shareholders, which starts with net income as reported. A bargain purchase gain recognized under ASC 805-30-25-2 is part of net income and is not excluded from the EPS numerator. Basic EPS = ($2,400,000 - $100,000) / 400,000 = $5.75. The analyst''s exclusion (option A) has no basis in ASC 260. There is no differential treatment between basic and diluted EPS for recognized income items (option D).', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Shares held by ESOP suspense account excluded
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, how are unallocated shares in a leveraged ESOP suspense account treated for basic EPS purposes?', '["Included in the weighted-average denominator as outstanding shares", "Excluded from the weighted-average denominator", "Included at 50% of their face count", "Included only if the ESOP debt is less than 80% repaid"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 718-40-45-5, unallocated (suspense) shares in a leveraged ESOP are not considered outstanding for EPS purposes and are excluded from the basic EPS denominator. Only allocated shares and shares committed to be released are included. Option A would overstate the denominator by treating unearned shares as outstanding. The rationale is that suspense shares have not yet been earned by employees and remain economically similar to treasury stock until released.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Tax benefit windfall from option exercise: no numerator add-back
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Employees exercise stock options that generate a $150,000 tax benefit windfall (excess tax benefit) for the employer. The employer recorded the benefit as a reduction in income tax expense under ASC 718-740-25. In computing diluted per-share data via the treasury stock method for those same options, should the $150,000 windfall be added to assumed proceeds?', '["Yes; the windfall is included in assumed proceeds under the treasury stock method", "No; the windfall is not an assumed proceed and remains in the per-share numerator through lower tax expense", "Yes; but only if the options are in the money at year-end", "No; the windfall must be excluded from both the numerator and assumed proceeds"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASU 2016-09 (effective 2017), ASC 260-10-45-29 was amended to remove excess tax benefits from the treasury stock method assumed proceeds. Post-amendment, assumed proceeds consist of the exercise price plus unamortized compensation cost only. Excess tax benefits now flow through income tax expense under ASC 718-740-25, increasing net income and thus the per-share numerator — they are no longer hypothetically recycled into assumed proceeds. The $150,000 windfall stays in the numerator through lower tax expense (option B). Option A reflects the pre-ASU 2016-09 treatment, which included excess tax benefits in assumed proceeds when they were credited to APIC. Option D is incorrect because the windfall properly remains in the numerator via reduced tax expense.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Cash-settled stock appreciation rights have no denominator effect
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity grants 20,000 stock appreciation rights (SARs) that will be settled entirely in cash. The SARs are liability-classified under ASC 718. The intrinsic value at year-end is $8 per SAR. What impact, if any, do these cash-settled SARs have on the diluted per-share denominator?', '["20,000 incremental shares are added via the treasury stock method", "No impact on the denominator because cash-settled SARs do not involve issuance of shares", "10,000 shares are added as an estimate of the net settlement equivalent", "The SARs increase the denominator by the intrinsic value divided by the market price"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28 and ASC 718-10-25, cash-settled SARs are accounted for as liabilities and settled in cash, not shares. Because no shares will be issued upon exercise, they have no effect on the diluted per-share denominator. The compensation cost is already reflected as an expense in net income (affecting the numerator). Option A incorrectly applies the treasury stock method, which is reserved for equity-settled instruments. Only share-settled SARs would add incremental shares.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Stock compensation expense reduces net income but does not change share count for basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A company recognizes $200,000 of stock-based compensation expense for unvested employee stock options, reducing net income to $800,000. The company has 200,000 weighted-average common shares. How does the stock compensation expense affect basic EPS?', '["It reduces the numerator to $800,000 and adds option shares to the denominator", "It reduces the numerator to $800,000 with no change to the denominator", "It has no effect on basic EPS because the expense is noncash", "It increases the denominator by the number of options granted"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-5 and ASC 718, stock-based compensation expense reduces net income (the numerator) but does not affect the weighted-average share count for basic EPS. Unvested options are not outstanding shares. Basic EPS = $800,000 / 200,000 = $4.00. Option A incorrectly adds option shares to the basic denominator; options affect only diluted EPS via the treasury stock method. Option C ignores that the expense was already deducted in arriving at net income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: easy / L2 — Down-round provision trigger: ASU 2017-11 deemed dividend recognition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A company''s freestanding equity-classified warrant contains a down-round provision. When triggered, the exercise price drops from $25 to $18. Under ASU 2017-11 (ASC 260-10-S99-2), what per-share amount is recognized on the trigger date?', '["No per-share effect because the warrant remains equity-classified", "A deemed dividend measured as the incremental fair value transferred to the warrant holder", "A gain recognized in the per-share numerator equal to the exercise price reduction", "An expense equal to the full fair value of the warrant at the new exercise price"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASU 2017-11 (codified in ASC 260-10-S99-2 and ASC 815-40-35-1A), when a down-round provision is triggered, the entity recognizes the value of the effect of the trigger as a deemed dividend — measured as the difference between the warrant''s fair value before and after the down-round adjustment. This deemed dividend reduces income available to common shareholders in the per-share computation. Option A ignores the required recognition. Option C and D mischaracterize the measurement; only the incremental value transfer is recognized, not the full fair value or the raw price change.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Deconsolidation of subsidiary: removing subsidiary results from per-share numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On September 30, a parent loses control of a subsidiary by selling a portion of its ownership stake. For the annual per-share computation, through what date should the subsidiary''s results be included in the consolidated numerator?', '["Through December 31 (full year), because the subsidiary was consolidated for part of the year", "Through September 30 only, because the parent includes subsidiary results only for the period of consolidation", "Not at all, because deconsolidation retroactively removes the subsidiary from all current-year per-share data", "Through September 30, and the gain or loss on deconsolidation is excluded from the per-share numerator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 810-10-40 and ASC 260, when a parent deconsolidates a subsidiary, the subsidiary''s operating results are included in the consolidated income statement only through the date control is lost. The numerator reflects subsidiary income through September 30. After that date, the former subsidiary is accounted for under the equity method (if significant influence is retained) or as an investment. The gain or loss on deconsolidation IS included in net income and therefore in the numerator. Option C incorrectly applies retroactive removal. Option D incorrectly excludes the deconsolidation gain/loss.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Correction of material misstatement restates previously reported per-share data
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'During 2026, management discovers a material inventory overstatement that inflated 2025 net income by $200,000. The 2025 financial statements reported basic per-share data of $4.00 based on $800,000 and 200,000 shares. When issuing comparative 2025/2026 financials, what must the entity do with the 2025 per-share figure?', '["Report the original $4.00 and disclose the error in the notes only", "Restate the 2025 per-share figure to $3.00 in the comparative presentation", "Report $4.00 for 2025 and reduce 2026 per-share data by the cumulative catch-up", "Restate only if the error exceeds 5% of net income"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-1 and ASC 260, a material prior-period error requires retrospective restatement. The corrected 2025 net income is $600,000. Restated per-share figure = $600,000 / 200,000 = $3.00. The comparative 2025 column must show $3.00, not the originally reported $4.00. Option A fails to restate. Option C incorrectly applies a cumulative catch-up to the current period rather than restating the affected period. There is no materiality threshold below which restatement is optional (option D).', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Subsequent event: shares issued after balance sheet date but before filing
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Between the balance sheet date and the financial statement issuance date, an entity sells 50,000 new common shares in a private placement. Under ASC 260-10-S99-3, what per-share disclosure is required?', '["The private placement must be retroactively reflected in the reported per-share amounts", "No action is required because the sale occurred after the reporting period", "The entity must disclose the transaction and its potential effect on per-share data in the notes", "The shares are included in the denominator at a half-year weighting"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-S99-3 (SEC staff guidance) and ASC 855 (subsequent events), a significant share issuance after the balance sheet date but before financial statement issuance requires disclosure. However, unlike stock splits and stock dividends, a share sale for consideration is NOT retroactively applied to the reported per-share amounts. The entity discloses the transaction and its per-share impact. Option A conflates a cash issuance with a capitalizing event. Option D fabricates a weighting method for post-period transactions.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Participating warrants with cash settlement alternative: classification drives EPS method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ferndale Corp. issues 10,000 warrants that entitle the holder to either (a) purchase one common share at $30 or (b) receive a cash payment equal to the intrinsic value. The average market price is $50. Net income is $2,000,000 with 400,000 weighted-average shares. The CFO applies the treasury stock method using 10,000 gross shares and a $30 exercise price. Under ASC 260-10-55-36, evaluate whether the CFO''s method is appropriate.', '["Correct; the treasury stock method applies to all warrants regardless of settlement alternatives", "Incorrect; the entity must assume the most dilutive settlement form, which may require a different method", "Incorrect; warrants with cash settlement alternatives are always excluded from diluted EPS", "Correct; but only if the entity has sufficient authorized shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-36 and ASC 260-10-45-45, when an instrument can be settled in shares or cash at the holder''s option, the entity must assume the most dilutive settlement form. If share settlement via treasury stock method produces 4,000 incremental shares [(10,000 - (10,000 x $30/$50)], but cash settlement would be more dilutive (e.g., $200,000 intrinsic value reducing the numerator with no denominator offset), the more dilutive method controls. The CFO may have applied the less dilutive approach. Option A ignores the dual-settlement analysis required by the standard.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Anti-dilution ratchet: using most favorable conversion ratio
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Blackwell Corp. has convertible preferred stock with an anti-dilution ratchet clause. Originally, each preferred share converts into 3 common shares. After a below-market equity issuance in 2025, the ratchet adjusts the conversion ratio to 4 common shares per preferred share. The adjusted ratio is effective immediately. For diluted EPS at December 31, 2025, which conversion ratio should be used?', '["3 shares per preferred share (the original ratio)", "4 shares per preferred share (the adjusted ratio)", "3.5 shares per preferred share (the average of both ratios)", "The ratio that produces the highest diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method uses the most favorable conversion rate available to the holder as of the reporting date. Since the anti-dilution ratchet has adjusted the conversion ratio to 4 shares effective immediately, the current ratio of 4 shares per preferred share is used for diluted EPS. Option A uses a superseded ratio. Option D would select the least dilutive result, which contradicts the most-favorable-to-holder principle. The most dilutive conversion terms from the holder''s perspective must be applied.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Dual-class structure: Class B super-voting shares and undistributed earnings allocation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A media company has two classes of publicly traded stock: 10 million Class A shares (1 vote each) and 2 million Class B shares (10 votes each) held by the founder. Both classes share equally in dividends on a per-share basis. Net income is $36 million and declared dividends total $12 million. An analyst assigns all undistributed earnings to Class A because it has more shares outstanding. Evaluate the analyst''s allocation methodology.', '["Correct; undistributed earnings follow share count, not voting rights", "Incorrect; undistributed earnings must be allocated based on voting power", "Incorrect; both classes participate equally per share, so undistributed earnings are allocated pro rata by share count — the analyst''s conclusion is right but for the wrong reason", "Incorrect; the two-class method allocates undistributed earnings based on each class''s contractual participation rights, yielding a per-share allocation to both classes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A and 60B, the two-class method allocates undistributed earnings based on each class''s contractual participation rights in earnings, not voting power. Since both classes share equally in dividends per share, undistributed earnings of $24 million ($36M - $12M) are allocated pro rata: Class A gets 10M/12M x $24M = $20M, Class B gets 2M/12M x $24M = $4M. The analyst''s error was assigning all undistributed earnings to Class A rather than allocating to both classes based on participation. Option B incorrectly ties allocation to voting power.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: hard / L3 — Net share settlement under ASU 2020-06: if-converted method required
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Blackmore Corp. issues $3,000,000 of 4% convertible bonds that permit net share settlement (cash for principal, shares for conversion spread). Under ASU 2020-06, the bonds are recorded as a single liability. The conversion price is $50 and the average market price is $65. Each $1,000 bond converts into 20 shares. The tax rate is 25%. Evaluate the diluted EPS treatment.', '["Apply the treasury stock method using the conversion spread only", "Apply the if-converted method to the entire instrument", "Exclude the bonds because principal is settled in cash", "Apply the treasury stock method to the full conversion shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASU 2020-06 (ASC 260-10-45-44A), all convertible instruments — including those permitting net share settlement — must use the if-converted method for diluted EPS. The prior option to use the treasury stock method for cash-settled principal instruments was eliminated. The after-tax interest is added back to the numerator ($120,000 x 0.75 = $90,000) and 60,000 potential shares are added to the denominator. Option A applies the now-eliminated treasury stock approach. Option C incorrectly excludes the instrument entirely.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Non-public entity: voluntary per-share presentation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A privately held company with no publicly traded securities voluntarily chooses to present per-share data in its audited financial statements. Under ASC 260-10-15-3, what standard must the entity follow?', '["The entity may use any reasonable method since ASC 260 does not apply to nonpublic entities", "The entity must comply with all provisions of ASC 260 if it chooses to present per-share data", "The entity may present only basic per-share data and omit diluted", "Per-share data presentation by nonpublic entities is prohibited under GAAP"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-3, if a nonpublic entity elects to present per-share data, it must compute and present that data in accordance with ASC 260. Voluntary presentation triggers full compliance with the standard''s computational and disclosure requirements. Option A incorrectly suggests the entity can use a non-standard method. Option C ignores the requirement to present diluted per-share data if dilutive securities exist. Option D is factually incorrect — nonpublic entities are permitted to present per-share data.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Penny warrants: nearly all gross shares are incremental
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A biotech firm grants 50,000 penny warrants (exercise price $0.01 each) to a strategic partner. The average market price of the firm''s stock during the reporting period is $40. Under the treasury stock method, approximately how many incremental shares result from the penny warrants?', '["50,000 because virtually no shares are assumed repurchased", "49,988 because assumed proceeds of $500 repurchase only 12 or 13 shares", "25,000 because assumed proceeds repurchase half the shares at $40", "Zero because sub-dollar exercise prices are disregarded"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method computes assumed proceeds as 50,000 x $0.01 = $500. Shares assumed repurchased = $500 / $40 = approximately 13 shares. Incremental shares = 50,000 - 13 = 49,987, which rounds to approximately 49,988. Because the exercise price is negligible relative to market price, nearly all warrant shares flow through as incremental dilution. Option A incorrectly assumes zero assumed repurchase. Option D has no basis — all non-zero exercise prices are included.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Subsidiary with own publicly traded securities: minority dilution
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A parent consolidates a 70%-owned subsidiary. The subsidiary has its own outstanding stock options that would add 10,000 incremental shares to the subsidiary''s diluted share count. Under ASC 260-10-45-26, how does the subsidiary''s potential dilution affect the parent''s consolidated diluted per-share data?', '["It is ignored because the parent''s per-share data uses only parent-level shares", "The subsidiary''s dilution reduces the parent''s interest in subsidiary earnings, lowering the consolidated numerator", "The 10,000 incremental subsidiary shares are added directly to the parent''s denominator", "It is reflected only if the subsidiary''s options are exercisable into parent shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-26, dilutive securities of a subsidiary included in consolidated financial statements are factored into the parent''s diluted per-share computation. The subsidiary''s potential dilution reduces the parent''s proportionate claim on subsidiary earnings — effectively lowering the consolidated numerator allocated to parent shareholders. Option A ignores subsidiary-level dilution entirely. Option C incorrectly adds subsidiary shares to the parent denominator; the effect flows through the numerator via the reduced ownership percentage.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Change in tax rate mid-year effect on convertible bond add-back
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgeway Corp. has $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds outstanding for the entire year, convertible into 25,000 shares. The statutory tax rate was 25% for the first half and changed to 21% for the second half. Interest expense was $30,000 each half. What is the correct after-tax interest add-back for diluted EPS?', '["$45,000 (using the 25% rate for the full year)", "$47,400 (using the 21% rate for the full year)", "$46,200 (using each half''s applicable rate)", "$60,000 (no tax adjustment needed)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the after-tax interest add-back reflects the actual tax benefit recognized in net income. First half: $30,000 x (1 - 0.25) = $22,500. Second half: $30,000 x (1 - 0.21) = $23,700. Total add-back = $22,500 + $23,700 = $46,200. Option A incorrectly applies the old 25% rate to the full year, yielding $45,000. Option B applies the new 21% rate for the full year. The correct approach uses each period''s enacted rate to mirror the tax expense actually recorded.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Basic and diluted EPS equal prominence on income statement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, how must basic and diluted EPS be presented relative to each other when an entity has a complex capital structure?', '["Only diluted EPS is required on the face; basic may appear in notes", "Both must be presented with equal prominence on the face of the income statement", "Basic EPS is primary; diluted may appear as supplementary data in notes", "The entity may choose to present either basic or diluted, but not both"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence on the face of the income statement. Neither measure takes priority over the other. Option A and C incorrectly subordinate one measure. Option D contradicts the dual-presentation requirement. The equal prominence requirement ensures that users of financial statements can evaluate both the current share-based performance (basic) and the potential dilution effect (diluted) without one measure being de-emphasized.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Equity method investee dilution gain: effect on parent per-share numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A parent owns 30% of an equity method investee. The investee issues new shares to third parties at a price above the parent''s per-share carrying amount, diluting the parent to 25% but generating a dilution gain. Under ASC 323-10-40-1, where does this gain appear in the parent''s per-share computation?', '["It is excluded from the numerator because it is unrealized", "It is included in the numerator as part of equity method income", "It bypasses the income statement and is recorded in other comprehensive income", "It is treated as a capital transaction and recorded directly in equity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 323-10-40-1, when an investee issues shares at a price above the investor''s per-share carrying amount, the resulting change in the investor''s proportionate interest can give rise to a gain. This gain is recognized in income (not OCI) and therefore flows into the per-share numerator. Option A incorrectly treats it as unrealized. Option C misclassifies it as OCI; ASC 323 routes this through the income statement. The gain reflects the investor''s enrichment from the investee''s equity issuance at a premium.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Leveraged recapitalization: new debt proceeds fund special dividend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'An entity completes a leveraged recapitalization, borrowing $50 million to fund a special cash dividend of $10 per share on its 5,000,000 outstanding shares. After the recap, the market price drops from $35 to $25 per share. Under ASC 260, how is the special dividend treated for per-share computation purposes?', '["As a stock dividend requiring retroactive adjustment of prior-period denominators", "As a return of capital with no per-share adjustment beyond reducing retained earnings", "As a large cash dividend; no retroactive adjustment of the denominator is required", "As a recapitalization requiring restatement of all historical per-share data"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, a cash dividend — even a large special dividend in a leveraged recap — does not affect the denominator because no new shares are issued or retired. The dividend reduces retained earnings and the numerator in the period declared, but does not trigger retroactive adjustment of prior-period denominators. Option A confuses a cash distribution with a stock dividend. Option D incorrectly treats a leveraged recap as requiring historical restatement. Only share-based distributions (stock splits, stock dividends) trigger retroactive denominator adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Warrants denominated in foreign currency: average exchange rate for treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A U.S. reporting entity issues 10,000 warrants with an exercise price of EUR 30 each. The average USD/EUR exchange rate during 2025 is 1.10 (EUR 1 = USD 1.10), making the USD-equivalent exercise price $33. The average market price of the entity''s stock is $55. Under the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares result?', '["10,000 shares (full gross amount because foreign-currency pricing is disregarded)", "4,000 shares using the $33 equivalent exercise price and $55 average market price", "6,000 shares using the spot rate at period end rather than the average rate", "Zero shares because foreign-currency warrants are excluded from diluted per-share data"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22 and ASC 260-10-55-36, warrants with exercise prices in foreign currencies are included in diluted per-share data. The exercise price is translated at the average exchange rate for the period (consistent with the average market price used in the denominator). Assumed proceeds = 10,000 x $33 = $330,000. Shares repurchased = $330,000 / $55 = 6,000. Incremental = 10,000 - 6,000 = 4,000. Option C incorrectly uses the spot rate; the average rate matches the average market price period. Option D fabricates an exclusion.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
